---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";

const moreInCategory = [
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-09", modified: "2026-09-09" });
const title = "6 Best Gas Generators for Home Backup in 2026: Tested";
const description =
  "Gasoline generators tested on a full household load — output, runtime and cold starts. Plus the fuel logistics that decide whether a gas generator works in a real outage.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "generac-gp8000e",
    "name": "Generac GP8000E",
    "category": "Gasoline, 8,000W Running / 10,000W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 4200,
    "pros": [
      "Fired on the first turn of the key every time, including at 34°F after a night outdoors",
      "8,000 running watts is the sweet spot for transfer-switch backup on a typical home",
      "Generac's dealer network means parts and service are genuinely available years later",
      "Hour meter with maintenance reminders — useful on a machine nobody keeps notes about",
      "Steel tube cradle and a covered outlet panel that tolerates being stored in a damp garage"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "73 dB under load, with a coarse engine note at full output",
      "Gasoline only, so the fuel storage problem is entirely yours to solve"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most reliable starter of the group and the easiest to get serviced a decade from now. The right default gasoline generator for home backup.",
    "description": "Starting reliability is the specification nobody publishes and every generator owner eventually cares about, and the GP8000E was the standout here. Across repeated warm and cold attempts, including a 34°F morning after sitting outdoors overnight, it fired on the first turn of the key without exception. For a machine that sits untouched for fifty-one weeks and is then asked to work in the worst weather of the year, that record outweighs a few hundred watts in either direction. The 8,000 running watts is arguably the ideal output for home backup: enough to carry an essential-circuit transfer switch including a furnace, sump pump, refrigeration and lighting, without the mass and thirst of a 12,000-watt machine. Generac's less glamorous advantage is the dealer network — published parts availability and service documentation are what determine whether this is repairable in year eight or landfill. The hour meter with maintenance reminders is a genuinely practical touch, since most owners have no idea when the oil was last changed. The limitation is inherent to the category rather than the machine. Gasoline only means you carry the whole fuel burden: stabiliser, rotation, cans, and a plan for refilling when every station within thirty miles has a queue or no power to run its pumps."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-wgen9500-gas",
    "name": "Westinghouse WGen9500",
    "category": "Gasoline, 9,500W Running / 12,500W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 12100,
    "pros": [
      "9,500 running watts for meaningfully less than comparably specified competitors",
      "Electric start, remote key fob and the battery included rather than sold separately",
      "Roughly 12 hours at half load — the longest gasoline runtime in this test",
      "Transfer-switch-ready outlets including the 30-amp twist lock a manual switch expects",
      "Very large owner base with a consistently strong long-term reliability record"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fuel gauge and control panel are basic and hard to read in the dark",
      "72 dB under load — better than some here, but no open-frame machine is quiet"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most watts per dollar in this test. If budget is a hard constraint and you still need real output, buy this one.",
    "description": "The WGen9500 is the value benchmark for gasoline home backup, delivering output within a few hundred watts of far pricier machines while including electric start, a remote fob and the battery rather than treating them as extras. On our household load it carried a refrigerator, chest freezer, furnace blower, cycling sump pump and lighting through a transfer switch with real headroom, and comfortably absorbed a window air conditioner on top. The figure that stood out was runtime: roughly 12 hours at half load from a full tank, the longest here, which matters more than it sounds. Refuelling happens in the dark, in bad weather, and only after the engine has cooled enough to be safe — a machine that clears a full night without that ritual is meaningfully easier to live with during a multi-day outage. The outlet configuration is properly thought through for home use, including the 30-amp twist lock that a manual transfer switch inlet expects, so it connects to a house the way it should rather than through a tangle of cords. The savings show up in refinement, not capability: the fuel gauge is awkward to read at night, the panel layout is functional rather than considered, and fit and finish sit a step below the premium machines. None of that affects whether it runs, and Westinghouse's reliability record across a very large owner base is genuinely strong."
  },
  {
    "id": "firman-p08004",
    "name": "Firman P08004",
    "category": "Gasoline, 8,000W Running / 10,000W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Size Build",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 3900,
    "pros": [
      "8,000 running watts with a heavy-duty frame and a genuinely usable wheel kit",
      "Electric start with a recoil backup that is easy to reach rather than an afterthought",
      "Around 12 hours at half load from an 8-gallon tank",
      "Covered, gasketed outlet panel — a real advantage on a machine stored outdoors",
      "Well-damped engine mounts that keep it from walking across a patio under load"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "74 dB under load; among the louder units here despite the damping",
      "Heavier than its output class suggests, at around 220 lbs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-built alternative to the Generac at a similar output, with better weather protection. Louder, and heavier than it needs to be.",
    "description": "The P08004 is the machine to look at if the generator will live somewhere damp and be run in bad weather, which describes most home backup units honestly. Its outlet panel is covered and gasketed rather than merely recessed, the frame is heavier gauge than the price suggests, and the engine mounts are damped well enough that it stays where you put it under load instead of gradually walking across a patio — a small thing that becomes irritating over three days. Output matched its 8,000-watt rating in testing, carrying our full essential load through a transfer switch with a window air conditioner added and margin remaining. Runtime came in around 12 hours at half load from its 8-gallon tank, competitive with the best here. Electric start worked reliably including at 34°F, and the recoil backup is positioned where you can actually get a straight pull at it, which is not true of every machine in this class — an important detail on the day the battery is flat. The costs are noise and mass. At 74 dB it was among the louder machines despite the damping, and at roughly 220 pounds it is heavier than its output class implies, so plan its position with a level path and expect it to stay there. As a conventional rather than inverter machine, sensitive electronics want a surge protector between them and the outlet."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-igen4500",
    "name": "Westinghouse iGen4500",
    "category": "Gasoline Inverter, 3,700W Running / 4,500W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Quiet Option",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 11800,
    "pros": [
      "52 dB at 23 feet under load — quiet enough to hold a conversation beside",
      "Clean inverter power suits laptops, TVs and modern appliance control boards directly",
      "Roughly 18 hours at quarter load from 3.4 gallons — by far the best fuel economy here",
      "Telescoping handle and wheels; genuinely one-person portable at around 100 lbs",
      "Electric start and a remote fob at a mid-range price"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "3,700 running watts covers essentials only — no central air, no well pump",
      "Roughly double the cost per watt of an open-frame gasoline machine"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it if noise or fuel economy is the deciding factor. Be clear-eyed that this is an essentials-only generator.",
    "description": "The iGen4500 answers a different question from the open-frame machines here: not how many watts you can buy, but how much generator you can live beside. At 52 dB under load it is quiet enough for a conversation nearby, which across a multi-day outage in a street of close-set houses is worth more than the specification implies. Inverter architecture does the work — the engine varies speed with demand instead of pinning at 3,600 rpm — and that produces two benefits at once. Noise falls dramatically at part load, and the output is a clean sine wave with low harmonic distortion, safe for laptops, televisions and the electronic control boards in modern furnaces and refrigerators without a line conditioner in between. Fuel economy follows from the same design, and it is the most consequential number on this page for a gasoline machine: roughly 18 hours at quarter load from 3.4 gallons. Over a three-day outage that is a fraction of the fuel an open-frame unit consumes, which directly reduces the number of cans you have to store, queue for, and carry. The constraint is output, and it is a hard one. At 3,700 running watts it carries a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, lighting and devices, and it will not simultaneously start a well pump or run central air. Buyers who talk themselves past that limit are the ones who end up disappointed."
  },
  {
    "id": "honda-eu2200i",
    "name": "Honda EU2200i",
    "category": "Gasoline Inverter, 1,800W Running / 2,200W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Small Generator",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 8900,
    "pros": [
      "48 dB at 23 feet — the quietest generator we have measured in any category",
      "47 lbs, carried in one hand and stored on a shelf rather than a garage corner",
      "Starting reliability that remains the benchmark for the whole small-engine industry",
      "Roughly 8 hours at quarter load from under a gallon of fuel",
      "Parallel a second unit for around 3,600 running watts when you need more"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1,800 running watts is a refrigerator, lights and devices — that is the honest ceiling",
      "Expensive per watt by a wide margin against every open-frame machine here"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best small gasoline generator made, and the wrong tool for whole-house backup. Buy it to keep food cold and the heating running.",
    "description": "The EU2200i is the most refined machine in this test and the least suited to the job most readers are shopping for, so it is worth being precise about what it is for. At 48 dB it is the quietest generator we have measured anywhere — quiet enough that neighbours often cannot identify the sound. At 47 pounds it is carried in one hand and stored on a shelf, which removes the storage and deployment friction that stops people using larger machines. Its starting record is the standard the rest of the industry is measured against, and its power quality is clean enough for medical devices and sensitive electronics without hesitation. Fuel consumption is correspondingly modest: roughly 8 hours at quarter load from under a gallon, which means a couple of five-gallon cans genuinely covers a long outage rather than a single night. What it will not do is back up a house. At 1,800 running watts you get a refrigerator, some lighting, device charging, and possibly a furnace blower if nothing else is drawing — and that is the ceiling, not a starting point. Two units in parallel reach roughly 3,600 running watts, which lands near the iGen4500 at considerably greater cost. Where it makes sense for home use is as a deliberate minimum: keeping food cold and the heat running, deployable by one person in ninety seconds, with none of the noise or storage burden of an open-frame machine."
  },
  {
    "id": "duromax-xp12000e-gas",
    "name": "DuroMax XP12000E",
    "category": "Gasoline, 9,500W Running / 12,000W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Maximum Output",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 5600,
    "pros": [
      "12,000 peak watts starts a central air compressor while already carrying a household load",
      "50-amp outlet lands straight into a transfer switch inlet with no adapters",
      "Copper alternator windings rather than aluminium, for better heat tolerance and life",
      "All-metal frame with the heft of a machine intended to last decades",
      "Ran a well pump alongside refrigerator, freezer and furnace simultaneously in testing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Over 260 lbs — decide where it lives once, because it is not moving again",
      "76 dB under load, the loudest machine in this test, and thirsty to match"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The gasoline portable that comes closest to standby coverage, air conditioning included. Big, loud, heavy and very capable.",
    "description": "The XP12000E is what you buy when the aim is covering essentially the whole panel rather than a chosen list of circuits. Its 12,000 peak watts handled the hardest test we ran — starting a central air conditioning compressor while already carrying a refrigerator, chest freezer, furnace blower and lighting — without tripping or noticeable voltage sag. That approaches what a small standby generator delivers at roughly a fifth of the installed cost, and for households in hot climates, where losing air conditioning is the actual emergency rather than an inconvenience, it is the reason to consider a large portable at all. Build quality matches the mission: an all-metal frame, copper windings rather than aluminium, and a general solidity that suggests it will still be running in fifteen years. The 50-amp outlet connects directly to a transfer switch inlet with no adapters, which is the only sensible way to use a machine of this output — extension cords cannot carry what it produces. The costs scale with the capability, and they are real. At over 260 pounds it is positioned once and left. At 76 dB it was the loudest unit here. And it is thirsty in a way that compounds the gasoline problem: at heavy load it will work through a five-gallon can considerably faster than the mid-size machines, which is worth planning for before the outage rather than during it."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Gas Generators",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-gas-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best gas generator for home use in 2026?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Generac GP8000E is our overall pick — the most reliable starter in our testing, 8,000 running watts that suits a typical transfer-switch setup, and a dealer network that keeps it serviceable years later. For the best value, the Westinghouse WGen9500 delivers more output for less. For quiet operation and much better fuel economy, the Westinghouse iGen4500 inverter.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a gas generator mean gasoline or natural gas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "In everyday use it almost always means gasoline — a portable machine you fill from a can. Natural gas generators are a different product entirely: usually permanently installed standby units plumbed into the house gas line, running indefinitely without refuelling. If you are shopping for something that connects to your gas meter, that is a natural gas or tri-fuel generator, not what most listings mean by gas.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much gasoline does a generator use per hour?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 7,000 to 9,000 watt open-frame generator burns roughly half a gallon to a gallon an hour at moderate load. A 3,500 to 4,500 watt inverter at part load can run on a quarter of that. Over a three-day outage the difference is stark: 20 to 40 gallons for the large machine against perhaps 8 to 12 for the inverter.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much gasoline should you store for a generator?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Enough for 48 to 72 hours of the load you actually plan to run, which for most mid-size machines means 20 to 30 gallons. Store it in approved containers, in a detached shed or garage rather than the house, treated with stabiliser, and rotate it into a car every three to six months so it never goes stale sitting in reserve.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does gasoline last in a generator?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three to six months in the tank with stabiliser, and as little as a month without it in warm conditions. Ethanol-blended fuel absorbs moisture and separates, and the residue varnishes the carburetor — which is the single most common reason a stored generator will not start when it is finally needed. Ethanol-free fuel stores considerably better.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a gas generator better than a dual fuel one?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A gasoline-only machine costs less and delivers its full rated output, since propane operation carries roughly a 10% derate. A dual fuel machine costs a little more and buys availability: propane stores indefinitely and sits on shelves after a regional storm, when petrol stations without power cannot pump. For emergency backup specifically, that availability usually outweighs the price difference.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run a gas generator continuously?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not indefinitely. Most run 8 to 14 hours per tank at half load, but the real limit is oil rather than fuel — plan a shutdown roughly every 24 hours to check and top it up. Refuelling also requires stopping and letting the engine cool, since pouring gasoline near a hot exhaust is one of the most common causes of generator fires.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size gas generator do you need for a house?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For essentials — refrigerator, freezer, lights and devices — 3,500 to 4,500 running watts. Adding a sump pump and a window air conditioner needs 5,500 to 7,000. Covering most essential circuits through a transfer switch takes 8,000 to 9,500. A well pump plus central air needs 10,000 to 12,000 running watts.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Home Fix Reviews" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "best gas generator",
      "gasoline generator",
      "gas generator for home backup",
      "portable generator",
      "emergency power"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Gas Generators</span> for Home Backup
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Gasoline machines deliver the most watts per dollar of any backup option. The question that decides whether one works is not output — it is how you get fuel on day three.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#generac-gp8000e"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#fuel-problem"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            The Fuel Problem
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#which-gas" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Gasoline or Natural Gas?</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#fuel-problem" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Fuel Problem</a>
          <a href="#storing-fuel" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Storing Gasoline Properly</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. What Size You Need</a>
          <a href="#vs-other-fuels" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Gas vs Propane vs Natural Gas</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Keeping It Startable</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Connection and Safety</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Gasoline generators win on price per watt and lose on logistics. Every one of these machines will run your house. Whether it is still running on day three depends on decisions you make before the storm arrives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="which-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. "Gas Generator" Means Two Completely Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            This guide covers gasoline generators — portable machines you fill from a can. That is what the phrase almost always means in practice, and it is what nearly every listing under the term is selling.
          </p>

          <p>
            A natural gas generator is a different product with a different job. Those are usually permanently installed standby units plumbed into the house gas line, which run indefinitely without anybody carrying a can anywhere, and cost several times as much once installation is included. If what you want is something connected to your gas meter, you are shopping in natural gas generators or tri-fuel machines, not here.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Distinction That Matters</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Gasoline: cheapest per watt, full rated output, and you own the fuel supply problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Natural gas: no refuelling ever, but a permanent installation and a much larger cheque. Propane: stores indefinitely and stays available after a storm, at roughly a 10% output penalty. Most households end up wanting gasoline plus a propane option, which is why dual fuel machines dominate the category.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 6 Best Gas Generators in 2026
          </h2>

