        <!-- SECTION 3 -->
        <div id="fuel-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Fuel Problem Nobody Plans For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the arithmetic that decides whether a gasoline generator actually works, and it is rarely on the box. A 7,000 to 9,000 watt open-frame machine burns roughly half a gallon to a gallon an hour at moderate load. Over a three-day outage that is 20 to 40 gallons — four to eight five-gallon cans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most households store one or two cans. The gap gets filled at a petrol station, except that a regional storm takes out the stations too: those without power cannot pump at all, and the ones running have queues measured in hours and limits on how much you can take. On day two of a widespread outage, buying 30 gallons of fuel is not a plan, it is a hope.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Machine</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Burn Rate</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">72-Hour Outage</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,200W inverter, part load</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~0.12 gal/hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-9 gallons</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4,500W inverter, part load</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~0.2 gal/hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-14 gallons</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8,000W open frame, half load</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~0.65 gal/hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-30 gallons</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12,000W open frame, heavy load</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.1 gal/hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35-45 gallons</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two conclusions follow, and both cut against the instinct to buy the biggest machine you can afford. The first is that an inverter's part-load fuel economy is not a minor efficiency statistic — it can be the difference between a fuel supply you can realistically store and one you cannot. The second is that running a 12,000-watt machine to keep a fridge cold is an expensive way to empty cans, so match the machine to the load, and run the big one only when the big loads are on.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also the strongest practical argument for dual fuel. Propane stores indefinitely, cannot go stale, and sits in exchange cages outside hardware stores after the pumps have failed. Four 20-pound tanks in the garage are days of essential power requiring no rotation and no queue — which is why our dual fuel testing exists as a separate guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <div id="storing-fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Storing Gasoline Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            If gasoline is your fuel, storage is the part of ownership that determines whether the machine starts. Aim for 48 to 72 hours of the load you actually plan to run — for most mid-size machines, 20 to 30 gallons.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Approved containers only</strong>, and stored in a detached shed or garage rather than inside the house or a basement.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Treat everything with stabiliser</strong> as you fill the can, not later. Untreated fuel can be unusable inside a month in summer heat.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Rotate every three to six months</strong> by pouring the oldest cans into a car and refilling. A dated marker on each can makes this a two-minute habit.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Prefer ethanol-free fuel</strong> where you can get it. Ethanol blends absorb moisture, separate in storage, and leave the deposits that varnish a carburetor.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Keep a funnel and a torch with the cans.</strong> You will be refuelling in the dark, and hunting for either in a blackout is how fuel gets spilled.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Fill up before the storm, not after.</strong> Every forecast of severe weather empties local pumps within hours.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Size Gas Generator Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running Watts</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Covers</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Price</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,800-2,200W</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Refrigerator, lights, devices, a furnace blower alone</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$500-1,200</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,500-4,500W</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fridge, freezer, furnace, lights and devices together</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-1,100</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,500-7,000W</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The above plus a sump pump and a window AC</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-1,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8,000-9,500W</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most essential circuits through a transfer switch</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$900-1,500</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10,000-12,000W</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Well pump plus central air — near whole-panel coverage</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,300-2,200</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Size against starting watts rather than running watts. Anything with a motor surges: a well pump drawing 1,200 running watts can demand 4,000 for a second, and a central air compressor is worse. Add the running watts of everything you want on simultaneously, then confirm the peak rating covers your largest motor's surge on top of that total. Our sizing guide works it through with real figures, and the wattage chart lists starting and running watts appliance by appliance.
          </p>

          <p>
            With gasoline there is a second consideration that does not apply to natural gas: every extra thousand watts you buy is fuel you have to store and carry. Oversizing a natural gas standby costs money once. Oversizing a gasoline portable costs you cans, every outage, forever.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="vs-other-fuels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gasoline vs Propane vs Natural Gas
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gasoline</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Propane</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Natural Gas</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Output</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full rated</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~10% lower</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~20% lower</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Storage life</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-6 months</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Indefinite</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Piped, none needed</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Runtime limit</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">What you can store</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tanks on hand</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Effectively unlimited</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Post-storm supply</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor — pumps need power</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good — tanks on shelves</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually uninterrupted</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold weather</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reliable</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Struggles below ~20°F</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unaffected</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upfront cost</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slightly higher</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Much higher installed</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Gasoline's genuine advantages are cost and full rated output, and in cold weather it is the most dependable of the three — propane's vaporisation falls off below roughly 20°F, which is precisely when winter outages happen. Its weakness is availability at the moment you need it most.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most households the honest recommendation is a dual fuel machine run primarily on gasoline, with propane held as the reserve that never goes stale. You keep the output and the cold-weather reliability, and you gain a supply that survives a regional outage. If the house already has a gas line and outages are frequent, the calculation shifts towards a standby unit instead — that comparison is in portable versus standby.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Keeping a Gasoline Generator Startable
          </h2>

          <p>
            A carburetor gummed by stale fuel is the single most common reason a stored generator will not start during an outage, and it is entirely preventable. Gasoline machines demand more of this discipline than any other type, because the fuel itself is what degrades.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise It Monthly, Under Load</h3>
          <p>
            Twenty to thirty minutes a month with something actually plugged in — a space heater or a couple of work lights will do. Running it unloaded is better than nothing but does not exercise the alternator or fully heat the engine. Put it in the calendar; every owner intends to do this and roughly half actually do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Decide How You Store It: Full or Dry</h3>
          <p>
            Either keep the tank full of stabilised fuel and run it through the carburetor so the whole system is treated, or shut the fuel valve with the engine running and let the bowl run dry before storage. Both work. What fails is the middle option — half a tank of untreated fuel sitting for eight months, which is how most no-start failures are created.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Is the Real Consumable</h3>
          <p>
            These engines hold about a quart, so oil degrades far faster than in a car and a missed change matters more. Change after the first 20 to 25 hours, then every 50 to 100 hours of run time. During an extended outage, check the level daily rather than trusting the interval — most units have a low-oil shutoff, and finding out it works at 3 a.m. on day two is avoidable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look After the Starting Battery</h3>
          <p>
            Electric start depends on a battery whose cranking capacity drops sharply in cold, so a battery that was fine in October can be inadequate in January. Keep it on a maintainer if the machine is stored unheated, and confirm the recoil backup actually works — many owners have never pulled it, and the day the battery is flat is the wrong time to discover it is seized.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Connection and Safety
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Backfeed, and Never Refuel Hot</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Connecting a generator to house wiring with a double-male cord energises the utility lines outside your home and can kill the crew repairing the outage, while bypassing every breaker in your panel. Use a transfer switch or an interlock kit. And never pour gasoline into a hot or running engine — fuel on a hot exhaust is one of the most common causes of generator fires, and it is a uniquely gasoline problem.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A transfer switch roughly doubles what a portable generator is worth, because extension cords only reach things with plugs. The furnace, the well pump, the sump pump and the lighting circuits — the loads that decide whether a house is habitable — are hardwired. A manual transfer switch runs $500 to $1,500 installed; an interlock kit is typically $200 to $500 and reaches every circuit in the panel. The trade-offs are in interlock kit versus transfer switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide is the other non-negotiable. Twenty feet minimum from any window, door or vent, exhaust pointed away from the house, and never in a garage — an open garage door does not make a garage outdoors. Fit battery-powered CO detectors inside the house as a second layer, and treat a built-in CO shutoff sensor as a backup rather than permission. Running one in bad weather is covered in running a generator in the rain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <PlanTheJob />

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas generator for home use in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Generac GP8000E — the most reliable starter in our testing, 8,000 running watts that suits a typical transfer-switch setup, and a dealer network that keeps it serviceable years later. For value, the Westinghouse WGen9500 gives more output for less; for quiet and much better fuel economy, the iGen4500 inverter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does "gas generator" mean gasoline or natural gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always gasoline — a portable machine you fill from a can. Natural gas generators are a different product: usually permanently installed standby units plumbed into the house line, running indefinitely without refuelling. If you want something connected to your gas meter, that is a natural gas or tri-fuel generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much gasoline does a generator use per hour?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 7,000 to 9,000 watt open-frame machine burns roughly half a gallon to a gallon an hour at moderate load. A 3,500 to 4,500 watt inverter at part load uses a quarter of that. Across a three-day outage that is 20 to 40 gallons versus perhaps 8 to 14.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much gasoline should you store?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enough for 48 to 72 hours of the load you plan to run — for most mid-size machines, 20 to 30 gallons. Use approved containers, store them in a detached shed or garage rather than the house, treat everything with stabiliser, and rotate into a car every three to six months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does gasoline last in a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three to six months with stabiliser, and as little as a month without it in summer heat. Ethanol blends absorb moisture and separate, leaving residue that varnishes the carburetor — the most common reason a stored generator will not start. Ethanol-free fuel stores considerably better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gas generator better than a dual fuel one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gasoline-only costs less and delivers full rated output, since propane carries roughly a 10% derate. Dual fuel costs a little more and buys availability — propane stores indefinitely and stays on shelves after a storm when stations cannot pump. For emergency backup, that availability usually wins.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run a gas generator continuously?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not indefinitely. Most run 8 to 14 hours per tank at half load, but the limit is oil rather than fuel — plan a shutdown roughly every 24 hours to check and top up. Refuelling also means stopping and letting the engine cool, since gasoline near a hot exhaust is a serious fire risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size gas generator do you need for a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Essentials need 3,500 to 4,500 running watts. Adding a sump pump and a window AC takes 5,500 to 7,000. Most essential circuits through a transfer switch need 8,000 to 9,500. A well pump plus central air needs 10,000 to 12,000 — and with gasoline, every extra thousand watts is fuel you have to store.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes With Gas Generators
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Untreated Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Half a tank of untreated gasoline sitting for eight months is how most no-start failures are created. Either store it full with stabilised fuel run through the carburetor, or shut the fuel valve and let the bowl run dry. The middle option is the one that fails.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning to Buy Fuel During the Outage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stations without power cannot pump, and the ones running have hour-long queues and purchase limits. A three-day outage on an 8,000-watt machine needs 20 to 30 gallons. Store it in advance, or buy a dual fuel machine and keep propane as the reserve.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refuelling a Hot Engine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline spilled on a hot exhaust is one of the most common causes of generator fires and burns. Shut it down and let it cool, every time — which is also why runtime per tank matters more than the spec sheet suggests.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It in a Garage With the Door Open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage is not outdoors, and carbon monoxide accumulates in one readily. This kills people every winter. Twenty feet from any window, door or vent, exhaust pointed away, with CO detectors inside the house as a second layer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying More Watts Than the Load Needs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With gasoline, oversizing is not a one-off cost — it is extra fuel to store and carry every outage. Running a 12,000-watt machine to keep a fridge cold empties cans fast. Match the machine to the load, and buy the headroom you genuinely need rather than the headroom on offer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Generator Guides</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">best gas generator</strong> for most homes is the <strong class="text-white">Generac GP8000E</strong> — the most reliable starter we tested, at the output that suits a typical transfer-switch setup, from a brand you can still get parts for in year eight. For value, the <strong class="text-white">Westinghouse WGen9500</strong>; for quiet and fuel economy, the <strong class="text-white">iGen4500</strong>; for maximum coverage including central air, the <strong class="text-white">DuroMax XP12000E</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then solve the fuel problem before the storm, because that is what actually decides whether a gasoline machine works. A three-day outage on a mid-size unit needs <strong class="text-white">20 to 30 gallons</strong>, stored in advance, stabilised and rotated — buying it during the outage is not a plan.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Exercise it monthly under load, store it either full and stabilised or completely dry, and fit a <strong class="text-white">transfer switch or interlock kit</strong> so it reaches the hardwired circuits that matter. Never backfeed, never refuel hot, and never run it within twenty feet of a window.
          </p>
        </div>


